comments.content:
The `dom` guard does not work for normal lib values. `compilerOptions.Lib` holds the raw `lib` entries from tsconfig (e.g. `["dom"]`, `["es2015"]` — see `tsoptions.libMap` and `internal/compiler/program.go`, which converts these with `GetLibFileName`; it never rewrites `compilerOptions.Lib`). So `slices.Contains(..., "lib.dom.d.ts")` is only true if the user literally wrote the file name (and is case-sensitive, while lib names are matched case-insensitively). Consequences:
- With `"lib": ["dom"]`, the guard never fires, so an empty/shadowed type named `Element`, `Node` or `HTML*Element` (e.g. a module-scoped `class Element {}`, which does not merge with the global interface) still gets the misleading "Try changing the 'lib' compiler option to include 'dom'" diagnostic even though dom is loaded.
- With `"noLib": true` (`Lib == nil` but dom is *not* available), the function short-circuits to `false` and misses the suggestion.
Suggest deriving "dom is loaded" from `NoLib` + the raw lib names, e.g.:

```go
if c.compilerOptions.NoLib != core.TSTrue {
	if c.compilerOptions.Lib == nil {
		return false // default lib includes dom
	}
	for _, lib := range c.compilerOptions.Lib {
		switch strings.ToLower(lib) {
		case "dom", "lib.dom.d.ts":
			return false
		}
	}
}
```

